.TH CHMAP_CREATE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chmap_create \- create a hash map with default settings
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap chmap_create(size_t initial_bucket_array_size,
                   ccol_data_type key_type,
                   ccol_data_type val_type,
                   char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a hash map with the default memory management and the default
hashing. Whether it uses open addressing or separate chaining is decided
from the key and value types.

Open addressing is used when both the key and the value are integral
types of <=8 bytes (long double excluded), and separate chaining for
non-integral types and types of >8 bytes.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
initial_bucket_array_size  Initial number of buckets. A value of 0 is
                           refused and the call fails; a value from 1
                           to 16 gives 16 buckets, and a larger value is
                           rounded up to the next power of two
key_type                   Type of keys (determines implementation)
val_type                   Type of values (determines implementation)
err                        Optional pointer that receives an error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A pointer to the new hash map on success, or NULL on failure.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The minimum bucket count is 16 (always a power of 2)
The default hashing depends on the key type and the hash mode, not on
the backend. A map starts in the fast mode, with a Fibonacci multiply
for an integral, float, double or pointer key and XXH64 with a secret
seed for a string or any other buffer-like key. It switches to a keyed
mode, with a seeded mixer and SipHash-1-3, when its keys collide more
than a random function would let them. Open addressing and separate
chaining behave the same here; chmap_create_full(3) describes the modes
and the thresholds
Call chmap_destroy() to destroy the map after use
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_create_full (3),
.BR chmap_create_mp (3),
.BR chmap_create_ch (3),
.BR chmap_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_determine_ccol_data_type (3)
